<ProjectLayout
  title="Applied security: this portfolio's own pipeline"
  description="How a Cybersecurity lens translates into practice: SAST, secret scanning, SBOM, and CSP applied to the very repository behind this site."
  domain="security"
  caseId="06"
>
  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>
    This case is a bit different from the others: instead of describing a corporate production
    environment, it describes the security of <strong
      >the very site you're looking at right now</strong
    >{' '}
    — because a postgraduate degree in Cybersecurity only has practical value if applied beyond projects
    explicitly labeled "security." The full code is public in the{' '}
    <a
      href="https://github.com/otaviohc19/otaviorodrigues"
      class="text-status underline decoration-status/40 underline-offset-2"
    >
      portfolio's repository
    </a>
    .
  </p>

  <h2>What runs on every push</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>SAST (CodeQL)</strong> — static code analysis on every push/PR and weekly, looking for vulnerability
      patterns before they reach production.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Secret scanning (Gitleaks)</strong> — ensures no key, token, or credential is ever committed,
      even accidentally — and even if removed later, Git history would preserve the secret if it wasn't
      caught in time.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dependency scanning (Dependabot)</strong> — weekly monitoring of dependencies with known
      vulnerabilities (CVEs), with rules to avoid mixing routine bumps with major versions that require
      manual migration.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>SBOM (Software Bill of Materials)</strong> — generated on every release, documenting exactly
      which libraries and versions make up the site, for traceability in case a new vulnerability is discovered
      in the future.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A conscious decision: CSP in an environment with no server control</h2>
  <p>
    The site is hosted on GitHub Pages, which doesn't allow custom HTTP headers. This limits the
    effectiveness of a real Content-Security-Policy — the alternative (CSP via{' '}
    <code>&lt;meta&gt;</code>) doesn't cover directives like <code>frame-ancestors</code>, which
    only work as a real HTTP header.
  </p>
  <p>
    The decision was to explicitly document that limitation instead of appearing to have security
    the hosting environment doesn't actually support — a principle that applies just as much to a
    personal portfolio as to corporate infrastructure: being honest about where the protection
    surface is weaker is more useful than pretending it doesn't exist.
  </p>

  <h2>Result</h2>
  <p>
    A publicly auditable pipeline, with real security checks instead of just claimed ones — anyone
    can open the repository's <em>Actions</em> tab and see the checks passing.
  </p>
</ProjectLayout>
